          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "My inhaler technique was never checked and I have had two courses of steroids this year. Is that a marker of poor control, and what does guidance describe about stepping up?",
          "answer": "You should have your inhaler technique assessed and discuss your response to current treatment with a clinician. Your history of two courses of steroids this year indicates that your asthma control may not be optimal. According to the Global respiratory strategy report and national asthma panel guidance, frequent courses of systemic steroids can signal poor control of asthma.\n\nBefore considering any changes to your treatment, it is crucial to evaluate your inhaler technique and adherence, as these factors significantly contribute to treatment failure. Many apparent failures can be addressed by improving inhaler use (Global respiratory strategy report and national asthma panel guidance).\n\nIf these aspects are satisfactory, the guidelines suggest various steps for \"stepping up\" asthma treatment. For lower steps, the recommendation includes using as-needed inhaled corticosteroids with a fast-onset long-acting bronchodilator or regularly scheduled low-dose inhaled corticosteroids. Middle steps involve regular low-dose inhaled corticosteroids combined with a long-acting bronchodilator, while higher steps may consider medium or high-dose combination therapy and further assessment as needed (from the Global respiratory strategy report). It is vital to have a discussion with your clinician to find the most appropriate treatment plan tailored to your needs.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
          "risk_level": "routine",
